::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which choice best defines active listening?
{
=A. Listening with attention, checking meaning, and responding thoughtfully#Correct. Active listening includes focus, understanding, and a useful response.
~B. Staying silent no matter what happens#Silence can be respectful, but active listening also requires understanding and response when appropriate.
~C. Planning your own answer while someone else talks#This divides attention. Active listening begins with understanding the speaker's message.
~D. Agreeing with every idea#Active listening does not require agreement. It requires respectful attention and response.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
In a planning discussion, a speaker says, "We need a stronger closing." Which response best supports collaboration?
{
~A. No, that is not important.#This shuts down the idea without listening or giving a reason.
~B. I do not want to talk about the closing.#This avoids the group's shared task.
=C. What kind of closing do you think would help the audience remember our point?#Correct. This question listens to the idea and invites the speaker to develop it.
~D. I already did enough work.#This centers the listener's frustration rather than the group's goal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response focuses on the idea instead of attacking the person?
{
~A. You always make confusing suggestions.#This attacks the person and uses an absolute claim instead of addressing the idea.
=B. I am not sure the example fits our topic because it does not connect to the main point.#Correct. This response gives a reason about the idea and keeps the discussion respectful.
~C. That is a terrible idea because you said it.#This attacks the person and does not explain a communication concern.
~D. Nobody should listen to you.#This is disrespectful and blocks collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A group member has not spoken during planning. What is the best collaborative move?
{
~A. Ignore the person because quiet people have no ideas.#This assumption is unfair and prevents full group participation.
~B. Tell the person they are ruining the group.#This attacks the person and does not invite communication.
~C. Finish the plan without them.#This may exclude a useful perspective.
=D. Ask, "What do you think would help our audience understand the topic?"#Correct. This invites participation and connects the question to the group's communication goal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which phrase is most appropriate for academic communication in a speech planning meeting?
{
~A. Your part is weird.#This is vague and personal. Academic communication should be specific and respectful.
=B. Your section has useful evidence, but it needs a clearer connection to the main idea.#Correct. This response uses precise language and gives actionable feedback.
~C. I hate this topic.#This expresses emotion but does not help the group improve the speech.
~D. Just make it sound better somehow.#This is too vague to guide revision.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which behavior would most likely harm a collaborative discussion?
{
=A. Interrupting each speaker before they finish#Correct. Interrupting prevents full listening and can stop useful ideas from being shared.
~B. Asking a clarifying question#Clarifying questions support understanding and collaboration.
~C. Paraphrasing a group member's idea#Paraphrasing shows active listening and helps check meaning.
~D. Inviting a quiet member to share#Inviting participation supports collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response best helps a classmate understand instructions?
{
~A. I am not explaining it.#This refuses to support communication.
~B. You should guess what the teacher means.#Guessing can lead to incorrect work. Instructional communication should clarify expectations.
~C. The directions say to post one claim and one reason; start with your claim first.#Correct. This points to the task and breaks it into a first step.
~D. Write more words so it looks finished.#This focuses on length, not understanding the assignment.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
What should a listener do before disagreeing with a speaker?
{
~A. Decide the speaker is wrong immediately.#This skips understanding and can lead to unfair disagreement.
~B. Change the topic quickly.#Changing the topic avoids the speaker's idea.
~C. Repeat the same disagreement louder.#Volume does not improve reasoning or listening.
=D. Make sure they understand the speaker's point.#Correct. Understanding the idea first helps the listener disagree respectfully and accurately.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best builds on another person's idea?
{
=A. Your point about audience attention connects to our opening; we could add a question there.#Correct. This connects the idea to the speech plan and adds a useful next step.
~B. I do not remember what you said.#This does not build on the idea because it does not show understanding.
~C. Stop talking about the audience.#This shuts down the topic instead of developing it.
~D. That has nothing to do with me.#This avoids the shared group task.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which statement best explains why collaboration matters in speech communication?
{
~A. It lets one person control all decisions.#This is the opposite of collaboration. Collaboration shares responsibility.
=B. It helps group members combine ideas, solve problems, and improve the final message.#Correct. Collaboration improves communication by using multiple perspectives toward a shared goal.
~C. It removes the need to listen.#Collaboration requires listening because group members must understand each other.
~D. It means every idea must be used.#Collaboration considers ideas, but not every idea has to be included.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response is a respectful disagreement?
{
~A. That idea is stupid.#This attacks the idea in a disrespectful way and gives no reason.
~B. You never help us.#This attacks the person and uses an absolute claim.
=C. I see the goal, but I think a shorter example would keep the audience focused.#Correct. This acknowledges the idea, gives a reason, and stays connected to the speech goal.
~D. I refuse to discuss it.#This stops the discussion rather than disagreeing constructively.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which choice uses the clearest academic language?
{
=A. The evidence supports our point, but we need to explain how it connects to the audience.#Correct. This uses precise communication words and gives a clear revision need.
~B. The stuff is kind of there.#This is vague. It does not identify what needs to improve.
~C. It is fine or whatever.#This is informal and does not provide useful communication feedback.
~D. Make the thing better.#This is too general to guide improvement.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A speaker says, "I am not sure our order makes sense." What is the best active listening response?
{
~A. The order is fine because I said so.#This dismisses the concern and gives no evidence.
~B. We do not have time for your concern.#This shuts down the speaker rather than addressing the message.
~C. I was not listening.#This directly shows a lack of active listening.
=D. You are concerned about organization. Which part feels out of order?#Correct. This paraphrases the concern and asks a clarifying question.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which choice is an example of turn-taking in discussion?
{
~A. Speaking over others until they stop#This prevents others from completing their thoughts.
=B. Waiting for a speaker to finish, then responding to the idea#Correct. This respects the speaker's turn and keeps the response connected to the discussion.
~C. Ignoring the topic and starting a new one#This breaks the flow of discussion.
~D. Letting only the loudest person decide#This does not support equal participation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
In a school setting, which response best supports social communication?
{
~A. Move, I need that seat.#This is abrupt and does not show respectful social communication.
~B. I do not care what anyone needs.#This blocks cooperation.
=C. Could we make space so everyone in the group can see the notes?#Correct. This politely identifies a shared need and supports group participation.
~D. Nobody else should talk.#This prevents communication and participation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which question best clarifies a speaker's message?
{
~A. Why are you always confusing?#This attacks the speaker instead of clarifying the message.
~B. Can you explain what you mean by "stronger evidence"?#Correct. This asks for the meaning of a specific phrase and helps the listener understand.
~C. Can we stop listening now?#This stops communication instead of clarifying it.
~D. Do you even know the topic?#This challenges the person in a disrespectful way.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which choice best shows that a listener has understood a speaker?
{
=A. The listener paraphrases the main idea accurately.#Correct. Accurate paraphrasing shows that the listener received and understood the message.
~B. The listener looks away and changes the topic.#This suggests the listener may not be focused on the message.
~C. The listener repeats one random word.#Repeating a word does not prove the listener understood the main idea.
~D. The listener disagrees before the speaker finishes.#This prevents full understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best helps solve a group disagreement?
{
~A. I win because I talked first.#This is about control, not collaboration.
=B. Let's list both options and decide which one better supports our audience.#Correct. This gives a fair process and connects the decision to the communication goal.
~C. We should stop working together.#This avoids solving the disagreement.
~D. The loudest person should choose.#Volume is not a good reason for a decision.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which response best uses academic vocabulary during feedback?
{
~A. It sounds bad.#This is vague and does not identify the communication issue.
~B. I do not like it.#This is an opinion without useful explanation.
~C. Your organization is clear, but the transition between points needs to be smoother.#Correct. This uses communication vocabulary and gives specific feedback.
~D. Just talk nicer.#This is too general to guide improvement.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A group member says, "I think our example is too long." Which reply is most collaborative?
{
~A. You are just trying to do less work.#This attacks motive instead of addressing the idea.
~B. I do not care about length.#This ignores the concern.
~C. Long examples are always better.#This uses an absolute claim without considering the audience.
=D. Let's check whether the example supports the main point or could be shortened.#Correct. This addresses the concern and suggests a useful next step.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
What is the main purpose of a respectful response?
{
=A. To address the idea in a way that keeps communication productive#Correct. Respectful responses focus on ideas and help the discussion continue.
~B. To avoid all disagreement#Respectful communication can include disagreement when it is handled with reasons and care.
~C. To make the other person stop talking#This blocks communication rather than supporting it.
~D. To prove the listener is smarter#This focuses on status, not shared understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response best supports a peer who missed a direction?
{
~A. You missed it, so that is your problem.#This is not supportive social or instructional communication.
=B. The direction was to submit one paragraph. I can show you where it says that.#Correct. This clarifies the task and helps the peer locate the instruction.
~C. Just submit nothing.#This does not help the peer complete the task.
~D. Ask someone else because I refuse.#This refuses communication support.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best shows a listener connecting ideas?
{
~A. I forgot what everyone said.#This does not connect ideas.
~B. Your idea has no connection to anything.#This dismisses the idea without analysis.
=C. Your point about practice connects to Mia's concern about sounding prepared.#Correct. This links two group members' ideas and helps the discussion become more organized.
~D. Let us stop planning.#This ends the discussion instead of connecting ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which choice is the best sign of collaborative communication?
{
~A. One person makes every decision alone.#This removes shared responsibility.
~B. Group members compete to speak the longest.#This focuses on control, not collaboration.
~C. Group members ignore questions.#Ignoring questions blocks understanding.
=D. Group members listen, respond, and build a shared plan.#Correct. Collaboration requires listening, responding, and working toward a common goal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best repairs a misunderstanding?
{
=A. I may have misunderstood. Did you mean that the introduction needs a clearer hook?#Correct. This admits possible misunderstanding and asks a specific clarifying question.
~B. You said it wrong, so it is your fault.#This blames the speaker and does not repair understanding.
~C. I will pretend I understood.#Pretending does not solve the communication problem.
~D. Your point does not matter.#This dismisses the message instead of repairing the misunderstanding.
}
